Keep the bound-texture cache right

GfxRenderingAPIOGL::SelectTexture remembers which texture each texture unit holds (mLastBoundTextures) and skips
glBindTexture when the texture asked for is already there. Three functions change what a unit holds without telling
that cache:
- CreateFramebuffer, and UpdateFramebufferParameters when a framebuffer drawn without MSAA gets a new size or MSAA
  level, bind the framebuffer's colour texture to set it up and then bind texture 0, on whichever unit is active.
- DeleteTexture deletes a texture; OpenGL then binds texture 0 on every unit that held it, and glGenTextures hands the
  deleted name out again.
The cache then still names the texture the unit held before, so the next SelectTexture of that name binds nothing:
whatever is drawn with it samples texture 0 and comes out black, and when Fast3D's texture cache, or NewTexture, gives
that name to a new texture, the new texels go into texture 0 instead and the name goes on showing the old ones.

A game rarely shows this, because it draws with many textures and the next different one binds the unit again. A
scene drawn with a single texture never recovers. Turnbuckle Dive's asset viewer, which draws one model with one
texture, showed it: stretching its window away from 4:3 makes Fast3D draw through a framebuffer of its own, which is
then sized again, and from then on the model was black. LibUltraShip's Gfx Debugger deletes and makes GUI textures
again in the same way.

The three functions now record that the units concerned hold texture 0.

Not in LibUltraShip main on 2026-11-09; drop this patch once it is there.

diff --git a/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp b/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp
index 6c4dfc86..c78fddc1 100644
--- a/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp
+++ b/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp
@@ -565,6 +565,13 @@ GLuint GfxRenderingAPIOGL::NewTexture() {
 
 void GfxRenderingAPIOGL::DeleteTexture(uint32_t texID) {
     glDeleteTextures(1, &texID);
+    // OpenGL binds texture 0 in place of a deleted texture on every unit that held it, and glGenTextures hands the
+    // name out again; SelectTexture must not skip binding it then.
+    for (GLuint& bound : mLastBoundTextures) {
+        if (bound == texID) {
+            bound = 0;
+        }
+    }
